From a3001eb0773109384409c7636f351f9ce621ff3c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 10:55:57 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] DMC11/DMR11: BOOT DMCn with a built-in MOP bootstrap
 (PDP-11); no crash on a transmit buffer too many

A real DMC11 or DMR11 has no boot of its own on the host's side: a local
boot runs from the CPU's boot ROM (DEC's M9312 DECnet ROM 'XM'), which
drives the board as a host program does.  SIMH has no such ROM, so
BOOT DMCn copies a small MOP bootstrap written for SIMH
(pdp11_dmc_boot.mac, position independent; pdp11_dmc_boot.h generated
from it) to the 8 KB below 160000 (or below the end of a smaller memory)
and starts it.  It master-clears the board, enters maintenance mode, gives
four receive buffers, sends Request Program (device type 12 DMC11 or 40
DMR11, the program type of SET DMCn PROGRAM), stores Memory Loads and
answers them with Request Memory Load, and starts the program at the
transfer address of a Memory Load or Parameter Load with Transfer Address
with R1 = CSR and SP = 1000, after a master clear.  Without an answer it
asks again after about 5 s (wait loops computed from the simulated
instruction rate); after three repeats, or on a load numbered 0, the load
starts over.  HELP DMC (Booting, BOOT command) and HELP BOOT describe it;
the 'XM' ROM route stays.

A BA/CC IN transmit command when no buffer is free dereferenced NULL and
crashed the simulator (also upstream); the command is now dropped
(known issue 115).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01SuavtcpUxEC2vXhit3JaAg
